When everyone is looking the other way, value investors start paying attention. 
@lululemon
 ($LULU) has lost nearly 75% of its market value over the past 18 months. Wall Street has largely turned bearish, with only a small fraction of analysts maintaining a Buy rating. Yet, Michael Burry—the investor who famously predicted the 2008 financial crisis—is increasing his position. His thesis isn't that Lululemon is flawless. It's that the market may be pricing the company as if its best days are over, while the fundamentals tell a more balanced story. Despite slowing growth, management challenges, and brand missteps, Lululemon remains: • Profitable • Debt-light • Cash-rich • A global premium brand with 800+ stores • Continuing to generate strong cash flows Burry believes the disconnect between price and intrinsic value has become unusually large, creating an opportunity for long-term investors willing to look beyond today's headlines. Whether he's right or wrong, one thing is clear: The best investments often emerge when fear dominates the narrative—not when optimism is everywhere. #LULU#Investing#Stocks#ValueInvesting#MichaelBurry#StockMarket#Finance#lululemon

Most investors treat a retailer's earnings miss as a company story. It is rarely just a company story. Lululemon beats on earnings. Misses on guidance. The stock drops. Nike beats on revenue. Margins compress. The stock drops. RH posts a loss. Warns on tariffs. The stock drops. Three different companies. Three different categories. Same direction. When the best-run consumer brands in the world start guiding lower at the same time, that is not a coincidence. That is a demand signal. Tariffs are compressing margins at the top line. Consumer confidence is softening at the bottom. And the companies caught in the middle, premium price points, discretionary spend, import-dependent supply chains, are the first to show the strain. The data does not have to come from a government report to be worth reading. Sometimes the most honest economic signal is a retailer telling you, quietly, that the customer is pulling back. 
